nt and the  government have to go  do you think that's going to happen  anytime soon  well the  last time that the  government or rather the people really  protested there was a big change uh the  prime minister left the time before that  uh on the 31st of march when again there  was a huge uh  upswelling of people's organic protest a  big change in governance a big change in  the people actually the ministers some  of the key economic technocrats who left  so there were there have been changes in  the past so i personally do feel that it  is looking increasingly likely that the  president will have to take some sort of  steps away uh now you're seeing mps from  the president's own party calling for  him to step down now we're saying the  prime minister calling for some sort of  new arrangement and it seems likely that  there is some change happening now you  can't really ignore the people's voice  when it's as loud as this when it's as  united as this so i do think there is  change coming and it's up to us to hope  and you know make sure that that change  will really do lead to an actual change  in the policies actually change in  reform how much of the blame for sri  lanka's current economic woes uh to the  president and his family uh bear here  so sri lanka's economy was always quite  fragile we had issues without a bunch of  deaths since we had issues with our  current account for a while but it's  with the graph of president and part of  those issues were exacerbated by  positive decisions taken over the last  15 20 years by the president's family  primarily uh his brother when he was uh  president that is mahindra rajapaksa but  over the last few years as this  government came in and this president  came in plenty of policy actions was  taken that significantly worsened the  underlying economic fragility of the  country made and really barreled the  country down to where we are right now  that includes massive tax cuts uh  between the countries already having 